{"title":"Euler's totient function","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Euler's totient function</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> applied to a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is defined to be the number of positive integers less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime\" title=\"Relatively prime\">relatively prime</a> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> is read \"phi of n.\"\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Formula\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Formula</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Derivation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Derivation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Identities\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Identities</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Graph\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Graph</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Notation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Notation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Formula\">Formula</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Formula\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given the general <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_factorization\" title=\"Prime factorization\">prime factorization</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/e/d0e9abae49533d86bf66d4429b2463d621a73936.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n} = {p}_1^{e_1}{p}_2^{e_2} \\cdots {p}_m^{e_m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"133\" height=\"17\" />, one can compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> using the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/0/c50d92c6f1a862f799eaf8a189db6acc7e2228ab.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\phi(n)= n\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_1} \\right) \\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_2} \\right)\\cdots \\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_m}\\right).\\]\" width=\"350\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><br />\nAlternatively, the formula can be represented as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/6/d96f262c7488a57f4e42233ba8249a029723c271.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\phi(n) = \\prod_{i=1}^{m} \\phi(p_i^{e_i}) = \\phi(p_1^{e_1}) \\cdot \\phi(p_2^{e_2}) \\cdot \\phi(p_3^{e_3}) \\dots \\phi(p_m^{e_m})\\]\" width=\"403\" height=\"47\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9b250a0aa2acd1acaf7d4554e30e7ec47f9c555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(p^k) = p^k - p^{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"142\" height=\"22\" /> for any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and for any positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Derivation\">Derivation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Derivation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To derive the formula, let us first define the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_factorization\" title=\"Prime factorization\">prime factorization</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/9/f795c95fec20d2e287e887fb8f65401e602a8afd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n =\\prod_{i=1}^{m}p_i^{e_i} =p_1^{e_1}p_2^{e_2}\\cdots p_m^{e_m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"204\" height=\"48\" /> where the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/245a5501248a6ea24f520f76d4140cedf08e1674.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"11\" /> are distinct <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number\" title=\"Prime number\">prime numbers</a>.  Now, we can use a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=PIE\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"PIE\">PIE</a> argument to count the number of numbers less than or equal to  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that are relatively prime to it.\n</p><p>First, let's count the complement of what we want (i.e. all the numbers less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that share a common factor with it).  There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/9/0a9712be145ce0ab0733d7ba4044a6d65bbf88be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{p_1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"19\" height=\"36\" /> positive integers less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/1/e01486dcc7cb25518b89692706b17389dc67c92b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" />.  If we do the same for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/245a5501248a6ea24f520f76d4140cedf08e1674.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"11\" /> and add these up, we get\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/6/576821b22ebb5e1a9c986631792e516828e99b1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{n}{p_1} + \\frac{n}{p_2} + \\cdots + \\frac{n}{p_m} = \\sum^m_{i=1}\\frac{n}{p_i}.\\]\" width=\"229\" height=\"47\" /></center>\n<p>But we are obviously overcounting.  We then subtract out those divisible by two of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/245a5501248a6ea24f520f76d4140cedf08e1674.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"11\" />.  There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/2/10240e118b3cff9851b7dd6d0d436f769de161b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{1 \\le i_1 &lt; i_2 \\le m}\\frac{n}{p_{i_1}p_{i_2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"123\" height=\"46\" /> such numbers. We continue with this PIE argument to figure out that the number of elements in the complement of what we want is\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/5/1a54da6486432515085420e1ba73b8010bf4351b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{1 \\le i \\le m}\\frac{n}{p_i} - \\sum_{1 \\le i_1 &lt; i_2 \\le m}\\frac{n}{p_{i_1}p_{i_2}} + \\cdots + (-1)^{m+1}\\frac{n}{p_1p_2\\ldots p_m}.\\]\" width=\"430\" height=\"43\" /></center>\n<p>This sum represents the number of numbers less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> sharing a common factor with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, so\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/b/74b32134c3513189214495d7cd0f6f964c8982c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n) = n - \\left(\\sum_{1 \\le i \\le m}\\frac{n}{p_i}- \\sum_{1 \\le i_1 &lt; i_2 \\le m}\\frac{n}{p_{i_1}p_{i_2}} + \\cdots + (-1)^{m+1}\\frac{n}{p_1p_2\\ldots p_m}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"545\" height=\"54\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/b/0abb2b2803bd171f2d4fa1f03db0e5a1edfa9a5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)= n\\left(1 - \\sum_{1 \\le i \\le m}\\frac{1}{p_i} + \\sum_{1 \\le i_1 &lt; i_2 \\le m}\\frac{1}{p_{i_1}p_{i_2}} - \\cdots + (-1)^{m}\\frac{1}{p_1p_2\\ldots p_m}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"540\" height=\"54\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/4/184b95ba7c3ac5c3e0ed2036c779643d7dc55ec4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)= n\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_1} \\right) \\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_2} \\right)\\cdots \\left(1-\\frac{1}{p_m}\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"350\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<ul><li>Note: Another way to find the closed form for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> is to show that the function is multiplicative, and then breaking up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> into its prime factorization.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Identities\">Identities</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Identities\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(a) For <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">prime</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/d/e1d2cd19e4f0ee37f273e6e5852284f152a5dad5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(p)=p-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"18\" />, because all numbers less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/7/b2714be477337f34f5ca1fb9ec98dc161a4f6ea4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> are relatively prime to it.\n</p><p>(b) For relatively prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/9/cd9ae36efddeb0482a3ac78d5fe97b4579345531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${a}, {b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/d/afd80dc554609f24b64e1d26695b1d4f8ab63b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi{(a)}\\phi{(b)} = \\phi{(ab)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"136\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Proof. The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a> states that for coprime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, there is a bijective (one-to-one) correspondence between an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/e/5ce8fda9e946b4927493410b403bf002df587a02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\pmod{ab}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" /> and the pair of residues <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/400db05819d44dc61f81564a3b11876dc102f0f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x \\pmod a, x \\pmod b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"189\" height=\"20\" />.\nAn integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/8/ef870ce9fefe09b80a97b05a7b9198414c1b35f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> if and only if it is relatively prime to both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> simultaneously.\n</p>\n<ul><li>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/6/d76d59c7dd64a2eac941d1ff1c4b00c691eb2eaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> integers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/5/c3567dc34deecde1da228a40cac5dd8c50940962.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{0, 1, \\dots, a-1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"128\" height=\"20\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/b/95ba45fb4da5e2d868c2f23b1a6a7746bafe4d98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x, a) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" />.</li>\n<li>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb0d4f69b22fd8b21a15ad702b11515fa1e066b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"35\" height=\"20\" /> integers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c4d66074c1e95332f67202f13fe906e7efa751.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{0, 1, \\dots, b-1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/4/0140cb0d612690cbbad26610e58332fd6810b815.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x, b) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>By the fundamental counting principle, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/6167e26390b836e00939aa4565858fa5b2c58d54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(a) \\cdot \\phi(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> such pairs, which corresponds exactly to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/4435e0e1c1ab358db32c4c21ad0c7aa213d8ecd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(ab)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> integers relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/8/ef870ce9fefe09b80a97b05a7b9198414c1b35f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/6/846c00abfb8d8731cc782000ccfb0f80aeff3a10.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\phi(ab) = \\phi(a)\\phi(b)\\]\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>(c) In fact, we also have for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/9/cd9ae36efddeb0482a3ac78d5fe97b4579345531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${a}, {b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/4/f34980a6ea170baa2de991737766281fdd3ff993.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi{(a)}\\phi{(b)}\\gcd(a,b)=\\phi{(ab)}\\phi({\\gcd(a,b)})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"294\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>(d) If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is prime and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/9/b892d5c2c417fa332d2cb127fb4af9bdfaaf67bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\ge{1},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d4d8a223a49969229520d6879dbef828729ad1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(p^k)=p^k-p^{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"142\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\nProof. To find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/131aec86b21eace7cc8117d6b91cb5f0545a6624.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(p^k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"22\" />, we count how many integers in the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a16d55c33eb61ae7e485486a9147efc9c69c01e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, \\dots, p^k\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"22\" /> are not relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/8/6585024dc6db82bcfd717e03212be80536d94a83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"19\" /> and subtract that count from the total. An integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is NOT relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/8/6585024dc6db82bcfd717e03212be80536d94a83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"19\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37e5d655c7ba16a219582978cb1827b62e847dc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x, p^k) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"22\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is prime, this only happens if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. The multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in the set are:<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/8/568121db1cc6ac2a59feb69d2a966dca10541371.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\{1p, 2p, 3p, \\dots, (p^{k-1})p\\}\\]\" width=\"184\" height=\"20\" />so we see that there are exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/c/a5cf3da08ae50508e5578698aa5954b4a818d950.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"21\" /> such multiples. Therefore:<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/a/82a3cf2f2e1b376abf9e31847062fd191eddd2fc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\phi(p^k) = (\\text{Total Elements}) - (\\text{Multiples of } p)\\]\" width=\"326\" height=\"20\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/1/ed1f36a17a4502a2a7a8db792a63f4eade066271.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\phi(p^k) = p^k - p^{k-1}\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n(e) For any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/0/300b1cdeb60210502daba0c895b8c804aa826be6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{d|n}\\phi(d)=n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"98\" height=\"42\" /> where the sum is taken over all divisors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Proof. Split the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/6/af6a6a7642d3100d9c213ea8226a95754d7ef900.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" /> into disjoint sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/6/5e63796243fd9eaedff18f21aaace9071239d340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> where for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/5/575cad109c7ca3561d5aee2883159d52f83ded76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/4/164794148c663188371f6512ad5b957bfb0c3fa8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_d=\\{x:1\\leq x\\leq n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\operatorname{syt}(x,n)=d \\}.\\]\" width=\"335\" height=\"18\" />\nNow <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed26937f3b8cfa2437bd0ebd91d793ea7f74a950.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{gcd}(dx,n)=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"18\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/7/90732ad356b2e449a8959869792a4dd4846ebe44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{gcd}(x,n/d)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />. Furthermore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/0/e603c1d2467a1e33560d5f2c843fbee4c5dec0b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq dx\\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"88\" height=\"15\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/0/a30124cb7169428d96758896c89adf1d4e4f2359.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq x\\leq n/d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" />. Now one can see that the number of elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/6/5e63796243fd9eaedff18f21aaace9071239d340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> equals the number of elements of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/9/289aca1cbdf1012697bd588c2a52a8deed7cd80a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_d^\\prime=\\{x:1\\leq x \\leq n/d\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\operatorname{gcd}(x,n/d)=1 \\}.\\]\" width=\"375\" height=\"18\" />\nThus by the definition of Euler's phi we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89f958228a3b942f397c4d767a071ab21cb699b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|A_d^\\prime|=\\phi (n/d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />. As every integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> which satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/d/20dc85bf0e34f8d7fbf66b886eedf3bee98aca45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq i\\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> belongs in exactly one of the sets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/6/5e63796243fd9eaedff18f21aaace9071239d340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" />, we have that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/d/70d92a847a91bf9cce283718263b6ab3815ffe57.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n=\\sum_{d \\mid n}\\phi \\left (\\frac{n}{d} \\right )=\\sum_{d \\mid n}\\phi (d).\\]\" width=\"204\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>(f) Another interesting thing to note is that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37fd57e08b7b7b3a1d21cb9b5e4e7bb4e8d976b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)\\leq n - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" />. This does seem very obvious but is helpful in solving many problems.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Graph\">Graph</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Graph\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Euler_totient_50000.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/e/ed/Euler_totient_50000.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"640\" height=\"480\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Notation\">Notation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Notation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Sometimes, instead of  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/1/921aade3100afbc11d0d13f71cdd7e68d83ad52e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"11\" height=\"11\" /> is used. This variation of the Greek letter <i>phi</i> is common in textbooks, and is standard usage on the English <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Wikipedia&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Wikipedia (page does not exist)\">Wikipedia</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video\">Video</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/T7INnve59JM\">https://youtu.be/T7INnve59JM</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_theory\" title=\"Number theory\">Number theory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">Prime</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_Totient_Theorem\" title=\"Euler&#39;s Totient Theorem\">Euler's Totient Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017133445\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.043 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.060 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 516/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 321/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 18226/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.590      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.590      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1729:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017133445 and revision id 278238. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}